:::
:::
:::::

## Why another notebook?

::::: columns
::: {.column width="50%"}
Jupyter notebooks are great for exploring, but the notebook you **see** is not always the program that **runs**

- Cells can be run in **any order**
- Deleting a cell **doesn't delete** its variables
- Widgets only update the cell they live in
- `.ipynb` is JSON with outputs inside, so **git diffs are noisy**
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
> In a study of ~1.4 million GitHub notebooks, only **24%** ran without errors and only **4%** reproduced their saved results
>
> Pimentel et al., *MSR 2019*

::: muted
marimo's answer: make the notebook a **reactive program**. Each cell declares what it reads and defines, and marimo keeps every output consistent with the code
:::
:::
:::::

## Hidden state, live

This slide is running marimo. **Move cell A**, then compare the two notebooks

:::::: columns
:::: {.column width="38%"}
```{python .marimo}
#| output: false
_rng = np.random.default_rng(1)
hba1c = np.round(_rng.normal(6.2, 1.3, 600).clip(4, 13), 1)
get_snap, set_snap = mo.state(6.5)
```

```{python .marimo}
thr = mo.ui.slider(4.0, 10.0, value=6.5, step=0.5, label="Cell A: threshold =",
                   show_value=True, full_width=True)
thr
```

```{python .marimo}
rerun_b = mo.ui.button(label="▶ Run cell B in Jupyter",
                       on_click=lambda _: set_snap(thr.value), kind="warn")
rerun_b
```

::: muted
- **Jupyter:** cell B only reruns when you run it
- **marimo:** cell B reruns as soon as `threshold` changes
:::
::::

::: {.column width="62%"}
```{python .marimo}
_stale = get_snap() != thr.value
_n_old = int((hba1c >= get_snap()).sum())
_n_new = int((hba1c >= thr.value).sum())
_code = (
    "```python\n"
    f"# A\nthreshold = {thr.value}\n\n"
    "# B\nn_flagged = (hba1c >= threshold).sum()\n\n"
    "# C\nprint(f\"{n_flagged} results ≥ {threshold}\")\n"
    "```"
)
mo.vstack([
    mo.md(_code),
    mo.hstack([
        mo.callout(mo.md(
            f"**Jupyter** · cell C prints\n\n`{_n_old} results ≥ {thr.value}`\n\n"
            + (f"⚠️ B last ran with threshold = {get_snap()}" if _stale else "✓ in sync")),
            kind="danger" if _stale else "success"),
        mo.callout(mo.md(
            f"**marimo** · cell C prints\n\n`{_n_new} results ≥ {thr.value}`\n\n"
            "✓ always in sync"), kind="success"),
    ], widths=[1, 1], gap=1),
])
```
:::
::::::

## How marimo runs a notebook

::::: columns
::: {.column width="44%"}
marimo reads each cell's code to find the **names it defines** and the **names it reads**. That gives a dependency graph (a DAG)

- Change a cell → marimo reruns **everything that depends on it**, and nothing else
- Each global name is defined in **one** cell
- Delete a cell → its variables are **deleted** too
- `_name` variables stay private to their cell
- UI elements (`mo.ui.*`) are just variables, so interacting with one reruns the cells that read `.value`
:::

::: {.column width="56%"}
```{mermaid}
%%| fig-width: 7
flowchart TD
    data["<b>data cell</b><br/>con, run(), CLAUSE"]
    ui["<b>widget cell</b><br/>yob = mo.ui.range_slider()"]
    sql["<b>SQL cell</b><br/>where_df = mo.sql(...)"]
    chain["<b>SQL cell</b><br/>SELECT … FROM where_df"]
    chart["<b>chart cell</b><br/>reads where_df"]
    data --> sql
    ui --> sql
    sql --> chain
    sql --> chart
    style ui fill:#FA4616,color:#fff,stroke:#FA4616
    style sql fill:#0021A5,color:#fff,stroke:#0021A5
    style chain fill:#0021A5,color:#fff,stroke:#0021A5
    style chart fill:#0021A5,color:#fff,stroke:#0021A5
```

::: muted
Move the slider (orange) and only the blue cells rerun
:::
:::
:::::

## Three file formats

::: panel-tabset
### Jupyter `.ipynb`

```json
{
 "cell_type": "code",
 "execution_count": 7,
 "id": "cell-12",
 "metadata": {},
 "outputs": [
  {"data": {"text/html": "<div><table border=\"1\" class=\"dataframe\">…"},
   "execution_count": 7, "output_type": "execute_result"}
 ],
 "source": ["person_preview = run(\"\"\"\n", "SELECT person_id, …\n", "\"\"\")"]
}
```

::: muted
JSON, with outputs and execution counts saved inside. Re-running a cell changes the file even when the code didn't change
:::

### Quarto `.qmd`

````markdown
## [WHERE]{.kw .where} filters rows

```{python .marimo}
yob = mo.ui.range_slider(1935, 2005, value=[1950, 1975], label="Year of birth")
yob
```

```{sql .marimo}
#| engine: con
SELECT person_id FROM person LIMIT 6
```
````

::: muted
Markdown plus code blocks. Quarto controls the layout; the marimo engine makes the blocks reactive
:::

### marimo `.py`

```python
@app.cell
def _(mo):
    yob = mo.ui.range_slider(1935, 2005, value=[1950, 1975], label="Year of birth")
    yob
    return (yob,)


@app.cell
def _(con, mo, yob):
    where_df = mo.sql(f"""
        SELECT person_id FROM person
        WHERE year_of_birth BETWEEN {yob.value[0]} AND {yob.value[1]}
    """, engine=con)
    return (where_df,)
```

::: muted
Plain Python. Function arguments are the cell's inputs, and return values are its outputs. Git-friendly, importable, and it runs with `python file.py`
:::
:::

## Three workflows {.center .uf-section background-gradient="linear-gradient(180deg, #002657 0%, #0021A5 100%)"}

Jupyter · Quarto · marimo

## Workflow 1: Jupyter

::::: columns
::: {.column width="45%"}
**Run it**

``` bash
uv run --with jupyterlab --with ipywidgets \
  --with anywidget --with duckdb --with pandas \
  --with numpy --with altair \
  jupyter lab sql-basics-jupyter.ipynb
```

**How the interactivity works**

- `@interact` builds the widgets and reruns **one function**
- Linked widgets need `.observe()` callbacks and an `Output` widget
- Chart selections go through `alt.JupyterChart` + `anywidget`
- The notebook includes a **hidden state** exercise
:::

::: {.column width="55%"}
``` python
@w.interact(
    yob=w.IntRangeSlider(value=(1950, 1975), min=1935,
                         max=2005, continuous_update=False),
    sex=w.Dropdown(options=[("Anyone", 0), ("Female", 8532),
                            ("Male", 8507)]),
)
def where_demo(yob, sex):
    sql = f"""SELECT person_id FROM person
              WHERE year_of_birth BETWEEN {yob[0]} AND {yob[1]}"""
    display(show_sql(sql), run(sql).head())
```

::: muted
The widgets and everything they affect must live in **one cell**. A second cell that reads `yob` will not update when you move the slider

[Open the notebook](sql-basics-jupyter.ipynb) · [Open in Colab](https://colab.research.google.com/github/{{< meta repo-path >}}/sql-basics-jupyter.ipynb)
:::
:::
:::::

## Workflow 2: Quarto + marimo engine

::::: columns
::: {.column width="45%"}
**Render it**

``` bash
quarto add marimo-team/quarto-marimo
quarto preview sql-basics.qmd
```

**How it works**

- `engine: marimo` runs each ` ```{python .marimo} ` block as a marimo cell
- The page is static HTML with **islands**: interactive widgets that run in the browser through **Pyodide** (Python compiled to WebAssembly)
- Quarto owns the layout: columns, slides, themes, SCSS, chalkboard
- Viewers can't edit the code
:::

::: {.column width="55%"}
``` yaml
engine: marimo
format:
  revealjs:
    theme: [default, omop.scss]
pyproject: |
  requires-python = ">=3.10"
  dependencies = ["marimo[sql]", "duckdb",
                  "pandas", "numpy", "altair"]
header: |
  import marimo as mo
  import duckdb
```

::: muted
Best for a **polished, narrated** lecture: everything this deck does, plus live widgets. The Pyodide runtime means larger data and some packages won't work in the browser

[Open the SQL deck](sql-basics.html)
:::
:::
:::::

## Workflow 3: native marimo

::::: columns
::: {.column width="45%"}
**Run it**

``` bash
# editable notebook, isolated env from the file header
uvx marimo edit --sandbox sql-basics-marimo.py

# same file as an app (code hidden)
uvx marimo run --sandbox sql-basics-marimo.py

# or just a script
uv run sql-basics-marimo.py
```

[Open the file](sql-basics-marimo.py) · [Open in molab](https://molab.marimo.io/github/{{< meta repo-path >}}/sql-basics-marimo.py)
:::

::: {.column width="55%"}
**What you only get natively**

- **SQL cells** with a schema browser; results are DataFrames you can query again
- **Table and chart selections** that drive other cells
- **Forms**, tabs, sidebar, data explorer, dataframe transformer
- A **dependency explorer** that shows the cell graph
- Packages declared in a **PEP 723** header at the top of the file
- The same `.py` file runs as a **notebook, an app, a script or a slide deck**
:::
:::::

## Same widget, three ways

::: panel-tabset
### Jupyter

``` python
@w.interact(yob=w.IntRangeSlider(value=(1950, 1975), min=1935, max=2005))
def where_demo(yob):
    sql = f"SELECT * FROM person WHERE year_of_birth BETWEEN {yob[0]} AND {yob[1]}"
    display(run(sql))        # output lives inside this function
```

### Quarto

````markdown
```{python .marimo}
yob = mo.ui.range_slider(1935, 2005, value=[1950, 1975])
yob
```

```{python .marimo}
where_sql = f"SELECT * FROM person WHERE year_of_birth BETWEEN {yob.value[0]} AND {yob.value[1]}"
mo.ui.table(run(where_sql))   # a separate block, updated reactively
```
````

### marimo

``` python
# cell 1
yob = mo.ui.range_slider(1935, 2005, value=[1950, 1975])
yob

# cell 2: a SQL cell; the result is a DataFrame
where_df = mo.sql(f"""
    SELECT * FROM person
    WHERE year_of_birth BETWEEN {yob.value[0]} AND {yob.value[1]}
""", engine=con)

# cell 3: SQL over the previous result
mo.sql("SELECT COUNT(*) FROM where_df")
```
:::

## Side by side

::: small
|  | Jupyter | Quarto + marimo | Native marimo |
|------------------|------------------|------------------|------------------|
| **File** | `.ipynb` (JSON + outputs) | `.qmd` (Markdown) | `.py` (Python) |
| **Execution** | Manual, any order | Reactive | Reactive |
| **Hidden state** | Yes | No | No |
| **Widgets across cells** | Callbacks | Automatic | Automatic |
| **SQL** | Strings in Python (or `%%sql` extension) | `{sql .marimo}` blocks, display only | SQL cells → DataFrames |
| **Where Python runs** | Local / remote kernel | Pyodide in the browser | Local kernel, molab, or Pyodide |
| **Viewer can edit** | Yes (with a kernel) | No | Yes |
| **Git diffs** | Noisy | Clean | Clean |
| **Share as** | nbviewer, Colab, Binder | Static website | molab link, WASM page, app |
| **Best for** | Ad-hoc exploration, existing ecosystem | Lecture decks, course sites | Labs, reproducible analyses, apps |
:::

## Sharing with molab

::::: columns
::: {.column width="48%"}
[molab](https://molab.marimo.io) is marimo's hosted notebook service: like Colab, but for marimo notebooks

- Point it at a notebook on GitHub
- **Static preview** loads instantly; *Run* starts a server
- Add `/wasm` to run it **in the viewer's browser**, with no login or server
- Packages install when they're imported
:::

::: {.column width="52%"}
**URL patterns**

``` text
https://molab.marimo.io/github/<owner>/<repo>/blob/<branch>/<path>.py
https://molab.marimo.io/github/<owner>/<repo>/blob/<branch>/<path>.py/wasm
```

**Badge** for a README or course page

``` markdown
[![Open in molab](https://marimo.io/molab-shield.svg)](https://molab.marimo.io/github/…/sql-basics-marimo.py)
```

::: muted
Commit `__marimo__/session/*.json` (from `marimo export session`) so the static preview shows outputs
:::
:::
:::::

## Moving between formats

```{mermaid}
%%| fig-width: 11
flowchart LR
    ipynb["sql-basics-jupyter.ipynb"]
    py["sql-basics-marimo.py"]
    qmd["sql-basics.qmd"]
    site["static site<br/>(HTML + WASM)"]
    molab["molab"]
    slides["reveal.js slides"]
    ipynb -- "marimo convert" --> py
    py -- "marimo export ipynb" --> ipynb
    py -- "marimo export html-wasm" --> site
    py -- "push to GitHub" --> molab
    qmd -- "quarto render" --> slides
    ipynb -- "quarto render" --> slides
    style py fill:#0021A5,color:#fff,stroke:#0021A5
```

::: muted
`marimo convert` turns `@interact` cells into marimo cells, but keeps the callback style. Compare the result with the hand-written `sql-basics-marimo.py`
:::

## Which one, when?

::::: columns
::: {.column width="33%"}
### Jupyter

- You're exploring on your own
- You need a Jupyter-only extension or kernel (R, Julia)
- Your team or platform already runs on `.ipynb`
:::

::: {.column width="33%"}
### Quarto

- You're presenting or publishing
- You need full control of the layout and theme
- Readers should see results, not edit code
:::

::: {.column width="33%"}
### marimo

- Students should **run and change** the analysis
- You want reproducible, git-friendly notebooks
- The notebook should also work as an app or a script
:::
:::::

::: muted
For this course: **teach from the Quarto deck**, and **practise in the marimo notebook**. The Jupyter version shows what reactivity saves you
:::

## Hands-on

1. Open the [Quarto deck](sql-basics.html) and move a few widgets
2. Open [`sql-basics-jupyter.ipynb`](sql-basics-jupyter.ipynb) and do the **hidden state** exercise
3. Open [`sql-basics-marimo.py`](https://molab.marimo.io/github/{{< meta repo-path >}}/sql-basics-marimo.py) on molab (or `uvx marimo edit --sandbox`), then
    - add a SQL cell that queries `where_df`
    - click a row in the JOIN table and watch the drill-down update
    - delete a cell and see what happens to the cells that used it

## References

These slides and notebooks are made using the following tools -

- [marimo](https://marimo.io/gallery) - a reactive Python notebook stored as a plain `.py` file, and [molab](https://molab.marimo.io) for hosting
- [Quarto](https://quarto.org/) - a scientific and technical publishing system built on Pandoc, with the [quarto-marimo](https://github.com/marimo-team/quarto-marimo) engine
- [Jupyter](https://jupyter.org/) and [ipywidgets](https://ipywidgets.readthedocs.io/)
- [DuckDB](https://duckdb.org/) - an in-process SQL OLAP database management system
- Pimentel, J. F., Murta, L., Braganholo, V., & Freire, J. (2019). *A Large-Scale Study About Quality and Reproducibility of Jupyter Notebooks.* MSR 2019
